1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:08,988
Take two phones, identical in every way. Charge one completely, drain
the other until it's dead,

2
00:00:09,008 --> 00:00:19,043
and set them on a balance. The charged phone is heavier. Not by any
amount you could measure.

3
00:00:19,063 --> 00:00:25,434
Yet nothing material went in while it charged. No atoms came down the
cable.

4
00:00:25,454 --> 00:00:34,088
No extra electrons piled up either: every electron that flowed in
through one wire flowed back out through the other.

5
00:00:34,108 --> 00:00:41,202
The only thing the phone gained was energy. And energy, it turns out,
has weight.

6
00:00:41,222 --> 00:00:46,929
Inside the battery, charging doesn't add stuff. It rearranges it.

7
00:00:46,949 --> 00:00:53,720
Ions shuttle between two layers into an arrangement that stores energy,
like a stretched spring.

8
00:00:53,740 --> 00:01:03,015
Use the phone, and they slide back down, releasing it. A full battery
and an empty one hold exactly the same atoms.

9
00:01:03,035 --> 00:01:09,053
The only difference is energy. So why would energy have weight at all?

10
00:01:09,073 --> 00:01:12,763
Matter is supposed to be the thing that weighs.

11
00:01:12,783 --> 00:01:22,003
For a century, science's most careful weighings said the opposite:
nothing inside a sealed container can change its weight.

12
00:01:22,023 --> 00:01:32,652
The rule was born in Paris, in the 1770s, with Antoine Lavoisier, a
chemist who trusted his balance more than any theory.

13
00:01:32,672 --> 00:01:40,232
He sealed tin inside a glass vessel, melting the glass shut so nothing
could get in or out, and took its weight.

14
00:01:40,252 --> 00:01:47,556
Then he heated it, until part of the shiny metal turned to grey powder,
let it cool, and took its weight again.

15
00:01:47,576 --> 00:01:52,360
Heat had poured in and out. The weight had not changed at all.

16
00:01:52,380 --> 00:02:02,742
In 1789, his rule: an equal quantity of matter, before and after.
Energy, it seemed, weighs nothing.

17
00:02:02,762 --> 00:02:06,746
But a rule is only as good as the balance that tests it.

18
00:02:06,766 --> 00:02:14,618
In the 1890s, Hans Landolt in Berlin spent 20 years testing it harder
than anyone.

19
00:02:14,638 --> 00:02:24,589
Two chemicals, sealed in the separate arms of a glass vessel, react; the
heat escapes; he weighs it again. And again. And again.

20
00:02:24,609 --> 00:02:28,854
He chased every error — buoyancy, moisture, dust.

21
00:02:28,874 --> 00:02:36,815
His 1908 verdict: no reaction changed weight by more than 0.03 mg.

22
00:02:36,835 --> 00:02:42,333
If the escaping heat weighed anything at all, it weighed less than that.

23
00:02:42,353 --> 00:02:47,628
Then a tiny glowing sample made that answer hard to believe.

24
00:02:47,648 --> 00:02:54,275
Radium, which Marie Curie purified in 1902, from more than a ton of ore.

25
00:02:54,295 --> 00:03:00,798
Pierre Curie measured one gram giving off a hundred calories of heat,
every hour.

26
00:03:00,818 --> 00:03:09,851
That adds up to nearly nine hundred thousand calories a year. A gram of
coal gives 7,000. Then it's gone.

27
00:03:09,871 --> 00:03:19,170
The radium releases a hundred times more, every year, and barely slows
down: half is still there sixteen hundred years from now.

28
00:03:19,190 --> 00:03:29,706
Nobody could say what it was using up. Either it soaked up invisible
energy from space, or the energy came from inside the atom itself.

29
00:03:29,726 --> 00:03:34,254
The first clue had appeared earlier, in the way light behaves.

30
00:03:34,274 --> 00:03:42,283
In 1900, Henri Poincaré worked through a fact most people never think
about: light pushes.

31
00:03:42,303 --> 00:03:49,971
It's why a comet's tail points away from the Sun; and lab experiments
had just measured that push.

32
00:03:49,991 --> 00:03:59,306
Anything that pushes carries momentum. A flash of energy E carries
momentum: E divided by the speed of light.

33
00:03:59,326 --> 00:04:08,857
Momentum is mass times speed. What mass would that flash need? Its
energy, divided by the speed of light squared.

34
00:04:08,877 --> 00:04:16,100
His example: a one kilogram lamp firing three million joules of light
recoils like a cannon,

35
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:23,533
while the flash carries thirty-three billionths of a gram. But he called
it a fiction, not a real weight.

36
00:04:23,553 --> 00:04:27,229
The number sat there. He didn't believe it.

37
00:04:27,249 --> 00:04:34,390
Believing it took Albert Einstein — 26, a clerk at the patent office in
Bern.

38
00:04:34,410 --> 00:04:44,868
In 1905, just after finishing relativity, he wrote to a friend: a body's
mass must be a measure of the energy inside it.

39
00:04:44,888 --> 00:04:54,570
Light carries mass, he said. Radium should get noticeably lighter. That
September, three pages in the leading physics journal.

40
00:04:54,590 --> 00:05:01,403
A glowing object, at rest, fires two identical flashes in opposite
directions.

41
00:05:01,423 --> 00:05:08,860
The pushes cancel. It just loses the light's energy, L. Now watch from a
passing train.

42
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:16,768
The speed is unchanged, but the forward flash now looks slightly more
energetic, the backward slightly less.

43
00:05:16,788 --> 00:05:23,804
Add them: most of the difference cancels. Not all. From the train, the
light carries away a little more than L.

44
00:05:23,824 --> 00:05:32,870
That extra must come out of its kinetic energy. And kinetic energy is
one half, times mass, times speed squared.

45
00:05:32,890 --> 00:05:41,652
The speed hasn't changed. So the mass drops — by the light's energy,
divided by the speed of light squared.

46
00:05:41,672 --> 00:05:48,294
A year later, Einstein offered a simpler argument: a flash of light
inside a box.

47
00:05:48,314 --> 00:05:53,577
Walk from one end of a small boat to the other, and it slides back under
you.

48
00:05:53,597 --> 00:05:59,104
Nothing inside a closed system can move its own centre of mass.

49
00:05:59,124 --> 00:06:05,098
Now make it a box in empty space, and make you a flash of light off the
left wall.

50
00:06:05,118 --> 00:06:15,355
The flash pushes the box back, crosses, and is absorbed at the right
wall — the box stops, shifted. Nothing outside touched it.

51
00:06:15,375 --> 00:06:21,633
So either the box pushed itself through space, or the light carried
mass, wall to wall.

52
00:06:21,653 --> 00:06:27,828
The boat settles it: box mass times shift equals light mass times box
length.

53
00:06:27,848 --> 00:06:35,937
Everything cancels but one line: the light's mass equals its energy,
divided by the speed of light squared.

54
00:06:35,957 --> 00:06:45,577
The left wall really gets lighter. The right wall really gets heavier.
Einstein credited Poincaré on the paper's first page.

55
00:06:45,597 --> 00:06:54,674
Once energy has real weight, the old mysteries make sense. Only one
thing hid that weight: the size of c squared.

56
00:06:54,694 --> 00:07:02,217
Light moves at three hundred million metres per second. So c squared is
ninety million billion.

57
00:07:02,237 --> 00:07:11,807
Ninety million billion joules, for one kilogram of weight. Landolt's
escaping heat weighed about a billionth of a gram.

58
00:07:11,827 --> 00:07:18,301
His balance could only see 30 millionths of a gram. Tens of thousands of
times too coarse.

59
00:07:18,321 --> 00:07:25,370
Even a gram of radium radiates a whole year of heat that weighs just
forty billionths of a gram,

60
00:07:25,390 --> 00:07:30,768
right at the edge of the finest balances — after twelve patient months.

61
00:07:30,788 --> 00:07:34,042
So could anyone weigh energy at all?

62
00:07:34,062 --> 00:07:42,851
It would take more energy per atom, and a way to weigh single atoms.
Both arrived in Rutherford's lab in Cambridge.

63
00:07:42,871 --> 00:07:53,976
Atoms were no longer weighed on pans: flying through magnetic fields in
a vacuum, the lighter ones curve more sharply. The bend became…

64
00:07:53,996 --> 00:08:02,036
On the 14th of April 1932, researchers fired fast protons at a thin
layer of lithium.

65
00:08:02,056 --> 00:08:09,236
One watched from a darkened hut beneath the machine, shielded from
X-rays. The screen lit up.

66
00:08:09,256 --> 00:08:15,541
Rutherford squeezed in: the flashes looked, he said, mighty like alpha
particles.

67
00:08:15,561 --> 00:08:21,662
A lithium nucleus had swallowed a proton and split into two helium
nuclei,

68
00:08:21,682 --> 00:08:30,640
flying apart with 17.2 million electron volts. Lithium plus hydrogen
outweighs two heliums.

69
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:36,726
If Einstein was right, the missing mass times c squared must equal that
energy.

70
00:08:36,746 --> 00:08:45,004
But lithium's 1932 mass predicted only 14 million electron volts,
against 17.

71
00:08:45,024 --> 00:08:54,168
A year later, a more precise lithium mass raised the prediction to 16.8
— within a few percent. It matched.

72
00:08:54,188 --> 00:09:04,285
Weighing atoms had already explained something bigger. In 1920, helium
proved lighter than the four hydrogens that build it.

73
00:09:04,305 --> 00:09:11,108
4.0313 against 4.0026.

74
00:09:11,128 --> 00:09:15,661
Arthur Eddington saw that the missing mass could power the stars.

75
00:09:15,681 --> 00:09:23,182
Fusing hydrogen into helium leaves seven tenths of one percent missing,
carried away as energy.

76
00:09:23,202 --> 00:09:33,264
The Sun fuses 600 million tons of hydrogen a second, and 4 million tons
of that never becomes matter — it leaves as light.

77
00:09:33,284 --> 00:09:38,663
The same equation runs the stars, and the battery in your pocket.

78
00:09:38,683 --> 00:09:46,934
So, back to the phones. The charged one gained energy, and therefore
mass: less than a billionth of a gram.

79
00:09:46,954 --> 00:09:56,493
No balance can show it. We know it anyway: every kind of energy weighs
exactly its amount, divided by c squared.

80
00:09:56,513 --> 00:10:02,942
Almost all the mass of your body is energy too, locked in its protons
and neutrons.

81
00:10:02,962 --> 00:10:11,891
The three quarks in a proton are less than one percent of its weight.
The rest is the strong force — trapped, seething motion.

82
00:10:11,911 --> 00:10:17,666
You are mostly stored energy, wearing the shape of a person. So is your
phone.

83
00:10:17,686 --> 00:10:23,570
Charge it tonight, and in the morning, it will be ever so slightly
heavier.

